# 8. Data, persistence and transaction integrity

## 8.1 System-of-record rules

Every data class must have one approved authoritative owner.

| Data | Authority |
|---|---|
| Account and tenant membership | CORE-001 |
| Lead and ownership | CORE-003 |
| Inventory and listing truth | CORE-004 |
| Search criteria and matching | CORE-005 |
| Deal and transaction state | CORE-006 |
| Trust and governance decision | CORE-007 |
| Commission and financial truth | CORE-008 |
| AI provider and model configuration | AI-001 under approved platform policy |
| Customer conversation and memory | Must be explicitly resolved through canonical ownership |
| Customer/business evidence | Must use an approved evidence owner |
| Developer execution continuity | ACRL and Control Center boundaries |
| Operational telemetry | Shared approved observability contract |

Derived indexes, caches, analytics and AI embeddings must be rebuildable from their approved sources.

## 8.2 Persistence implementation

Before adding persistence code:

1. Re-read ARCH-037 and the approved schema.
2. Inspect the existing repository and migration paths.
3. Identify the aggregate's canonical owner.
4. Identify the transaction boundary.
5. Confirm tenant enforcement.
6. Confirm uniqueness and referential integrity.
7. Define migration compatibility.
8. Define recovery and reconciliation.
9. Add focused repository and integration tests.
10. Verify backup and restore for release-critical data.

No second ORM model set may silently become a competing schema.

## 8.3 Tenant database enforcement

Where the approved PostgreSQL architecture uses Row-Level Security:

- RLS must be enabled and tested on applicable tables.
- Application connections must use non-owner, non-bypass roles.
- Tenant context must be established server-side.
- Transaction-local context must not leak across pooled connections.
- Missing tenant context must fail closed.
- Cross-tenant negative tests must run against the actual database.
- Administrative bypass must be explicit, restricted and audited.

Application-level filters alone are insufficient proof of tenant isolation.

## 8.4 Financial integrity

Financial operations must preserve:

- Fixed-precision decimal semantics.
- Explicit currency.
- Approved rounding policy.
- Balanced double-entry invariants where applicable.
- Immutable posted entries.
- Reversal and adjustment instead of destructive mutation.
- Idempotent settlement.
- Reconciliation against external evidence.
- Separation of estimate, authorization and settlement.
- Traceability across commission, payment and dispute lifecycles.

Floating-point arithmetic must not be the canonical financial representation.

## 8.5 Migration and recovery

Every migration must specify:

- Version and checksum.
- Preconditions and postconditions.
- Expected data transformation.
- Locking and availability impact.
- Backward compatibility.
- Failure behavior.
- Rollback or forward-fix.
- Tenant and financial integrity checks.
- Post-migration verification.

A successful schema migration without data integrity verification is not complete.

